---
// Figure 6: skewed reads across shards, and why adding shards stops helping.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Slider from '../shared/Slider.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import Readout from '../shared/Readout.astro';
import './cluster.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="cluster-hotshard" title="One hot key, and the shard that holds it" controls="none"
  caption="Illustrative model, not a measurement. 600,000 reads a second spread over 100,000 keys by a Zipf distribution, each key placed by its real hash slot on shards that own equal ranges of slots; each node is assumed to serve 100,000 reads a second. Left out: writes, replicas, network limits, and the way a real overloaded node slows down rather than stopping at a line.">
  <Fragment slot="params">
    <Slider name="shards" label="Shards" value={12} stops={[3, 6, 12, 24, 48]} unit="shards" />
    <Choice name="skew" label="Skew of reads (Zipf s)" value="1"
      options={[{ value: '0.8', label: '0.8 (milder)' }, { value: '1', label: '1' }, { value: '1.2', label: '1.2 (steeper)' }]} />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="stats">
    <Readout name="busiest" label="Busiest node, reads a second" kind="redis" />
    <Readout name="usable" label="Usable share of total capacity" kind="hit" />
    <Readout name="ceiling" label="Most the cluster can take" kind="write" />
    <Readout name="top" label="The hottest key alone" kind="neutral" />
  </Fragment>
  <div data-columns></div>
  <p class="cluster-hot-key" aria-hidden="true"><i></i>reads of the single hottest key, which stay on one node however many shards there are<span><b>✕</b> a node over its capacity</span></p>
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Each column is one shard's read rate, and the solid block at the foot of one column is the hottest key's share. With the reads spread as Zipf with s = 1, the hottest key alone receives about 49,600 of the 600,000 reads a second. With 3 shards the busiest takes 250,000 against an average of 200,000, so the cluster can use 80% of its capacity. At 6 shards the busiest falls to about 138,000 while the average halves to 100,000. With 12 shards the average falls to 50,000 but the busiest still takes about 100,000, so half the capacity is usable; with 48 shards the busiest takes about 75,000 against an average of 12,500, and only 17% is usable. The busiest column shrinks only as far as the hottest key plus the other popular keys that share its node, and the hottest key's 49,600 never shrinks. The busiest node fills first, so 48 nodes that could serve 4.8 million reads a second between them can take only about 800,000 of this workload, and no number of shards takes the cluster past about 1.2 million, where the hottest key alone would fill its node.</p>
    <p>With s = 1.2 the hottest key alone receives about 118,000 reads a second, more than one node can serve, so no number of shards is enough. With s = 0.8 it receives about 13,000 and adding shards keeps helping for longer.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { bindParams, setReadouts } from '../shared/player.mjs';
  import { columnChart } from './columns.mjs';
  import { nodeLoads, CAPACITY } from './hotshard-model.mjs';

  const k = (v: number) => v >= 1e6 ? `${(v / 1e6).toFixed(2)} million` : `${Math.round(v / 1000)}k`;
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="cluster-hotshard"]').forEach(root => {
    const chart = columnChart(root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-columns]')!, {
      label: 'Reads a second arriving at each shard, with one node’s capacity as a line', capacity: CAPACITY, top: 320000,
    });
    bindParams(root, (params: any) => {
      const r = nodeLoads({ shards: Number(params.shards), s: Number(params.skew) });
      chart.set(r);
      setReadouts(root, {
        busiest: k(r.max),
        usable: `${Math.round(r.usable * 100)}%`,
        ceiling: `${k(Math.min(r.ceiling, r.nodes * CAPACITY))} a second`,
        top: `${k(r.topKeyReads)} a second`,
      });
    });
  });
</script>
